    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The single most common way a good epoxy floor gets ruined is not a dropped wrench or a dragged toolbox. It is a homeowner mopping it every month with a citrus degreaser or a splash of vinegar, and wondering two years later why the mirror finish turned into a flat, chalky gray. Epoxy is chemically tough and mechanically hard, but its gloss lives in the top few thousandths of an inch, and acid takes it away permanently.
        </p>

        <div id="what-epoxy-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What You Are Actually Cleaning</h2>
          <p>
            A finished epoxy garage floor is not one material. In a typical system there is a primer bonded into the concrete, one or two pigmented epoxy build coats, often a broadcast layer of vinyl flake, and finally a clear topcoat — usually polyurethane or polyaspartic on a quality install, or a second pass of epoxy on a budget kit. Everything you see, touch, walk on and clean is that topcoat. The colored layer underneath is doing structural work; the clear layer is doing all the appearance work.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters because cleaning damage is nearly always topcoat damage. A floor that looks worn out after three years usually has a perfectly intact epoxy body with a scratched, hazed or etched clear coat sitting on top. The good news is that this is the cheapest possible failure to fix — a scuff sand and a fresh coat of clear restores it completely for a fraction of what a full recoat costs. The bad news is that most people do not realize the damage is happening until the whole floor has gone uniformly dull, at which point the cause is long forgotten.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second thing worth understanding is that epoxy is non-porous. Concrete absorbs; epoxy does not. Nothing you spill on an intact coating soaks in, which is the entire reason the floor exists. Every stain is sitting on the surface, and every cleaning problem is a surface problem. This is why aggressive chemistry is almost never necessary, and why aggressive chemistry does far more harm than the stain it was aimed at.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-4 mil</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical clear topcoat thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">7.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Target pH for routine cleaning</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-20 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Life of a well-maintained pro system</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Max PSI we would use on a coating</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="routine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Maintenance Routine That Actually Works</h2>
          <p>
            Epoxy maintenance is boring by design. Three habits cover ninety percent of it, and the frequency matters more than the effort put into any single session.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekly: Dust Mop or Blow Out</h3>
          <p>
            The thing that scratches an epoxy floor is grit. Sand, road salt crystals, and the fine aggregate that falls off a car underbody act like sandpaper under tires and shoes, and every pass grinds a little more gloss off the topcoat. A microfiber dust mop takes four minutes for a two-car garage and removes essentially all of it. A leaf blower does the same job faster if the garage opens onto a driveway, though it moves the grit rather than collecting it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you do only one thing on this list, do this one. Abrasion is the dominant wear mechanism on garage epoxy, and dust mopping is the only maintenance step that directly addresses it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Monthly: Wet Mop With a Neutral Solution</h3>
          <p>
            Warm water with either a pH-neutral floor cleaner at label dilution or about half a cup of clear ammonia per gallon. Use a microfiber flat mop or a well-wrung string mop, work in sections, and change the water when it turns gray. There is no need to rinse an ammonia solution; it evaporates and leaves nothing behind, which is exactly the property you want.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is where most people go wrong by reaching for dish soap. Dish soap works, but it leaves a surfactant film that dries slick and dulls the reflection. Each wash adds another layer, and after a year the floor looks cloudy and feels dangerously slippery when wet. If you have been mopping with soap, one deep clean usually reveals a floor that looks nearly new underneath.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Immediately: Spot Clean the Aggressive Spills</h3>
          <p>
            Motor oil, gasoline, and antifreeze are harmless to a cured epoxy floor for a short time and harmful over a long one. Brake fluid and battery acid are in a different category entirely — brake fluid softens many coatings within hours, and battery acid will eat through a topcoat and into the pigmented layer if left overnight. Wipe up any of these when you see them, then clean the spot with your neutral solution.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Winter Rule</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Road salt and de-icing brine are the hardest thing a residential garage floor deals with. The brine that drips off a car in February is corrosive, and once the water evaporates the salt crystals remain as abrasive grit. Through the salt season, rinse or mop every two to three weeks rather than monthly. A cheap rubber-backed containment mat under each vehicle cuts the problem dramatically.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="deep-clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Annual Deep Clean, Step by Step</h2>
          <p>
            Once a year — most sensibly in spring, after the last salt has come off the roads — the floor benefits from a full wash rather than a mop. Budget two hours for a two-car garage and do it on a day warm enough that the floor dries within an hour or two.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clear and Sweep</h3>
          <p>
            Move everything out, including the shelving if it is not fixed. Sweep or blow the entire slab, paying attention to the perimeter where grit accumulates against the wall and to the corners of the vehicle bays. Any grit left behind gets dragged around by the brush in the next step and puts fine scratches into the topcoat.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Pre-Treat the Tire Tracks</h3>
          <p>
            Spray a simple degreaser onto the wheel paths and any oil shadows and let it dwell for ten minutes. Dwell time is what does the work here; scrubbing immediately after spraying wastes both the product and the effort. While it sits, treat any individual stains you have identified using the guidance in section six.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Wash With a Stiff Nylon Brush</h3>
          <p>
            Flood a section with your cleaning solution, work it with a stiff nylon deck brush on a pole, and move on before it dries. Nylon is the key word — it is aggressive enough to break the bond between the topcoat and whatever film is on it, and soft enough that it cannot scratch cured epoxy. Never use a wire brush, a green scouring pad, or any abrasive powder.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Rinse Thoroughly and Squeegee</h3>
          <p>
            Rinse with a hose, or a pressure washer at low pressure and a wide tip, then squeegee the water out the door. Squeegeeing is not optional if you care about appearance: water left to evaporate on a floor deposits every dissolved mineral it was carrying, and hard water leaves a visible haze that looks exactly like a failing topcoat. Pull the water off, let the film that remains dry, and the floor should come back to full gloss.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Inspect While It Is Clean</h3>
          <p>
            A clean, dry floor is the only time you can honestly assess the coating. Look for chips at the door threshold, hairline cracks telegraphing up from the slab, edges lifting near the apron, and any area where the gloss no longer matches. Small chips can be spot-repaired with a compatible patch kit. Widespread dullness across the wheel paths but nowhere else is the classic sign that a clear coat has worn thin and should be refreshed before the color layer starts taking damage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Cleaners Compared</h2>
          <p>
            Almost every question about cleaning epoxy comes down to one decision: what goes in the bucket. Here is how the realistic options compare.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[520px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cleaner</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Safe on Epoxy</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear ammonia, half cup per gallon</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Routine mopping, rinse-free</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">pH-neutral floor cleaner</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Routine mopping, flake floors</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based alkaline degreaser</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, rinse after</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil film, tire tracks, deep cleans</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dish soap</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harmless but residual</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing — leaves a slick film</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinegar or citrus cleaner</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — etches gloss</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Muriatic acid or concrete etch</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — destroys the coating</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bare concrete only, before coating</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Ammonia deserves its reputation as the default. It cuts oil film, evaporates completely, leaves no residue to build up, and costs almost nothing. The one caution is the obvious one: never mix it with bleach, and ventilate the space while you work. If the smell is a problem, a commercial pH-neutral cleaner does the same job with less complaint, and the concentrate is cheap enough per gallon of working solution that the premium hardly registers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="never-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What Never to Use on Epoxy</h2>
          <p>
            This is the section worth remembering, because everything here causes damage that cleaning cannot undo.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Anything Acidic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vinegar, lemon juice, citrus cleaners, phosphoric acid rust removers, and every product sold as a concrete etch. Acid attacks the topcoat resin and leaves a permanent chalky dullness. The damage is often uneven, which looks worse than uniform wear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Strong Solvents Left to Dwell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A quick wipe of mineral spirits on a rag to lift a tire mark is fine. Pouring lacquer thinner, acetone or brake cleaner onto the floor is not — those soften the coating and can leave a permanently soft, tacky patch that collects dirt forever.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Steel Wool, Wire Brushes and Scouring Pads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                All of them cut the topcoat. Steel wool has the added problem of leaving fine steel fragments behind that rust into orange freckles across an otherwise clean floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">High-Pressure Washing at a Narrow Tip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A zero-degree or fifteen-degree tip at 3,000 PSI will lift coating edges, drive water beneath the film at any crack or chip, and strip flake out of a decorative floor. Low pressure with a wide fan is genuinely useful; the turbo nozzle is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rock Salt Spread on the Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some people salt the garage floor to melt what the car brings in. Salt is corrosive to the coating and to any exposed concrete at the edges, and it turns into abrasive grit as the water evaporates. Use a containment mat instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Specific Stains and How to Handle Them</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Tire Marks</h3>
          <p>
            The dark patches under where the tires sit are not dirt, and scrubbing harder does not help. When a tire heats up on the highway, plasticizers migrate out of the rubber and stay soft; when the hot tire parks on a coating, that material transfers and grabs. On a properly installed floor with a good topcoat, it lifts with a degreaser and a nylon brush. On a thin one-part garage floor paint, hot tire pickup can actually pull the coating off the concrete, which is a coating problem rather than a cleaning problem.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Motor Oil and Transmission Fluid</h3>
          <p>
            Blot the puddle, then degrease. Because the coating is non-porous, nothing has soaked in and the stain is entirely on the surface. Fresh spills come off completely; a drip that sat for months may leave a faint shadow in the gloss where the oil slightly softened the topcoat, and that shadow is permanent.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rust Rings From Tools and Jack Stands</h3>
          <p>
            A metal object left on a damp floor leaves an orange ring. Resist the reflex to buy a rust remover, because nearly all of them are acid-based and the cure is worse than the stain. Try a nylon brush with your normal cleaner first, then a magic-eraser style melamine pad used gently. Accept a faint shadow rather than etching the floor to chase it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paint, Adhesive and Dried Drywall Mud</h3>
          <p>
            Let it dry fully, then lift the edge with a plastic scraper and pop it off. Cured epoxy releases dried latex paint and joint compound easily. Use a plastic putty knife or the plastic blade of a razor scraper, never a steel blade — a steel edge will gouge the topcoat in one pass.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Acid</h3>
          <p>
            The one true emergency. Neutralize immediately with baking soda, let it fizz out, sweep up the residue, then wash the area with your neutral cleaner. Acting within minutes usually saves the coating; leaving it overnight almost never does, and the repair is a spot recoat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Mops, Brushes and Machines</h2>
          <p>
            The tool list is short and cheap. A microfiber dust mop with a wide head for the weekly pass, a microfiber flat mop or a good string mop for wet cleaning, a stiff nylon deck brush on a pole for annual scrubbing, and a floor squeegee for pushing rinse water out the door. That is the whole kit, and it will last years.
          </p>
          <p>
            A shop vacuum earns its place for the corners and for wet pickup if the garage has no slope to the door. A pressure washer is useful once a year at a wide tip and modest pressure — think of it as a fast rinse, not a scrubbing tool. Where slabs slope inward or the floor drain is inside the building, a wet vacuum is genuinely better than a hose, because standing water that dries in place is what leaves mineral haze.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walk-behind auto scrubbers, the machines commercial shops use, are excellent on epoxy and complete overkill for a residential garage. If you do rent one, fit a soft white or red pad and a neutral solution. The aggressive black stripping pads that come with rental machines are designed to remove floor finish, and on epoxy they remove gloss.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more note on floor mats: solid rubber mats without breathing room can trap moisture and, on some coatings, cause staining or plasticizer interaction over months. Ribbed or textured containment mats that let air move underneath avoid this, and they are the right answer for catching winter brine anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cleaner for an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pH-neutral floor cleaner diluted in warm water, or roughly half a cup of clear ammonia per gallon of hot water. Both lift oil film and road salt without attacking the coating. Avoid anything acidic, anything containing citrus or pine oil solvents, and any soap-based cleaner that leaves a slippery residue.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use vinegar on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Vinegar is acetic acid, and repeated use etches the topcoat, leaving a dull, chalky haze that no amount of buffing brings back. The same applies to any citrus-based cleaner or acidic concrete etch product. Once the gloss is gone the only remedy is recoating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, at low pressure with a wide fan tip held well back from the surface. Keep it under about 1,500 PSI, use a 40-degree tip, and never hold the nozzle close enough to see the water bite. High pressure at a narrow angle can lift edges, drive water under the coating at cracks, and blast flakes out of a decorative floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you get tire marks off an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hot tire marks are plasticizer transferred from the tire, not dirt. Scrub with a stiff nylon brush and a degreaser, or use a small amount of mineral spirits on a rag for stubborn spots, then rinse thoroughly. Do not use a wire brush or an abrasive pad, and do not let solvent sit on the coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you clean an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dust mop weekly, wet mop monthly, and do one deep clean each spring after the salt season ends. Spot clean oil, brake fluid, antifreeze and battery acid immediately — those are the spills that actually damage a coating, and the damage is a function of how long they sit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my epoxy floor still slippery after mopping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always soap residue. Detergent films dry into a slick layer that gets worse with every wash. Rinse with clean water after any soap-based cleaner, or switch to an ammonia or pH-neutral solution that rinses clean. If the floor is slippery when wet by design, an anti-slip additive in the next topcoat is the real fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will cleaning restore the shine to a dull epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the dullness is film. A deep clean removes soap haze, road film and tire residue, and floors often look dramatically better afterward. If the gloss loss is abrasion or acid etching in the topcoat itself, cleaning cannot help — that surface needs a scuff sand and a fresh clear coat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Mistakes That Shorten a Coating's Life</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mopping With Soap Forever</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cumulative film is the most common cause of a floor that looks tired at three years. It is also the easiest to fix — one ammonia wash usually takes the haze straight off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Grit Live on the Floor All Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every drive in and out grinds that grit into the topcoat under a two-ton load. Sweeping is more valuable than any product you can buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dragging Jack Stands and Toolboxes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Point loads on steel feet cut through a topcoat instantly. Put plywood or rubber pads under anything heavy that moves, and lift rather than drag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Until the Color Layer Shows Wear to Recoat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clear coat refresh on a dull but intact floor is a weekend and a couple of hundred dollars. Once wear reaches the pigmented layer, you are looking at a full grind and reinstall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

