        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Cleaning an epoxy garage floor</strong> is a five-minute weekly habit and a two-hour annual wash, and the products involved cost less than a tank of gas. Dust mop the grit before it grinds, mop with ammonia or a neutral cleaner rather than soap, rinse and squeegee so nothing dries in place, and treat oil and battery acid the day they land.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Everything that actually destroys these floors comes from the other direction: <strong class="text-white">acid, solvent, abrasive pads, and high-pressure water</strong>. Keep those four away from the topcoat and a properly installed system will still look good long after the car parked on it is gone.
          </p>
